,"Terms with Russian translations","Terms with Scots translations","Terms with Shor translations","Terms with Spanish translations","Terms with Swedish translations","Terms with Tamil translations","Terms with Thai translations","Terms with Turkish translations","Terms with Ukrainian translations","Terms with Uyghur translations","en:Federal subjects of Russia","en:Khakassia, Russia","en:Places in Russia","en:Republics of Russia"],"links":[["republic","republic"],["Russia","Russia#English"],["Republic of Khakassia","Republic of Khakassia#English"],["Abakan","Abakan#English"]],"glosses":["A republic and federal subject of central south Russia. Official name: Republic of Khakassia. Capital: Abakan."],"id":"c1b05d97-3331-4194-9a6f-5b2d4d9bcaf1"}],"related":[{"word":"Khakass"},{"word":"Khakas"},{"word":"Khakassian"}],"synonyms":[]},"Khabarovsk":{"id":"6a427aa4-58e9-463e-90ce-84d1c0931829","word":"Khabarovsk","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"city in Russia","tags":["feminine"],"word":"Khabarovsk"}],"pos":"name","tags":null,"senses":[{"categories":["en:Khabarovsk Krai, Russia","en:Krais of Russia","en:Places in Russia"],"links":[["krai","krai"],["Russia","Russia#English"],["Russian Far East","Russian Far East"]],"glosses":["A krai of Russia, in the Russian Far East."],"id":"d8b4b720-4f62-447d-86ff-9596a1c491bb"},{"examples":[{"text":"The Governor-General of Amur resides at Khabarovka (哈巴羅甫喀，即，伯利), in the Primorsk Province (東海濱省).","ref":"[1904, C. D. Tenney, “俄屬 [RUSSIA IN ASIA]”, in Geography of Asia, New York: MacMillan and Co, →OCLC, page 36:","english":"RUSSIA IN ASIA","type":"quotation","translation":"RUSSIA IN ASIA","bold_text_offsets":[[40,50]],"id":"8b1adbc6-2da1-4e27-9577-8903f6282bf9"},{"text":"The distance between China's Heihe harbor and the Soviet city of Hailanpao is only 1.5 km and the distance between Tongjiang harbor and the largest city in the Soviet Far East, Khabarovsk, is only 270 km.","ref":"1986 October 6, Zhu Guang, “Use of Overall Compensation in Sino-Soviet Border Trade”, in China Report: Economic Affairs, Foreign Broadcast Information Service, →OCLC, page 28:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[177,187]],"id":"65be13d1-bd05-4ae9-85c8-1f2edc1aa909"},{"text":"The major obstacle to a border settlement is the status of Heixiazi, a 330-square kilometer island at the confluence of the Amur and Ussuri rivers. The island is claimed by the Chinese but controlled by the USSR. Because Heixiazi overlooks Khabarovsk as well as the point where the Trans-Siberian Railway crosses the Amur River, the Soviets are reluctant to give it up.","ref":"1991, Donald S. Zagoria, “The Changing Role of the Soviet Union in the Pacific”, in Frederic J. Fleron, Jr., Erik P. Hoffmann, Robbin F. Laird, editors, Contemporary Issues in Soviet Foreign Policy: From Brezhnev to Gorbachev, New York: Aldine de Gruyter, →ISBN, →LCCN, →OCLC, page 678:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[240,250]],"id":"ce0a9173-f231-461b-8411-049fa25fb8a0"},{"text":"Heihsiatzu Island, the most strategically important of the Amur islands, is situated at the juncture of the Amur and Ussuri Rivers and screens Khabarovsk from Manchuria. Here too, the main channel had shifted from south to north of the island.³² Khabarovsk was the second largest city in the Soviet Far East and the administrative center of the Far Eastern Army. If the channel north and east of Heihsiatzu Island were recognized as the boundary, Japanese naval vessels would be within their rights to steam right up to the city’s docks and Japanese artillery on the island would have the ability to fire at point-blank range into the city.","ref":"2012, Stuart D. Goldman, “The Global Context”, in Nomonhan, 1939: The Red Army's Victory That Shaped World War II, Annapolis, Maryland: Naval Institute Press, →ISBN, →LCCN, →OCLC, page 32:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[143,153],[246,256]],"id":"149e622a-f5e2-40f5-9e4f-45ec6c3e15c8"},{"text":"People’s anger over their depleted fish stock is so widespread that it has been a driving force behind the anti-Kremlin protests that have been shaking the Far Eastern city of Khabarovsk, on the Amur, since early July.","ref":"2020 August 15, Anton Troianovski, “'The Fish Rots From the Head': How a Salmon Crisis Stoked Russian Protests”, in The New York Times, →ISSN, →OCLC, archived from the original on 15 Aug 2020:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[176,186]],"id":"066c3527-039d-4119-8fda-bee4e347bce7"}],"categories":["English terms with quotations","en:Cities in Khabarovsk Krai, Russia","en:Cities in Russia","en:Krai capitals of Russia","en:Places in Khabarovsk Krai, Russia","en:Places in Russia"],"links":[["city","city"],["Khabarovsk Krai","Khabarovsk Krai#English"],["Russia","Russia#English"]],"glosses":["A city, the administrative center of Khabarovsk Krai, Russia."],"id":"d057a56d-3620-46fe-9a2c-863caf4164fc"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[{"sense":"from Mandarin Chinese","word":"Boli"},{"word":"Achansk"},{"word":"Khabarovka"}]},"Khanty-Mansiysk":{"id":"e37420fa-3373-4daf-8005-9a0c1f01765e","word":"Khanty-Mansiysk","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"city in Russia","tags":["feminine"],"word":"Khanty-Mansiysk"}],"pos":"name","tags":null,"senses":[{"categories":["English entries with incorrect language header","English lemmas","English multiword terms","English proper nouns","English terms borrowed from Russian","English terms derived from Russian","English uncountable nouns","Entries with translation boxes","Kango terms in nonstandard scripts","Pages with 2 entries","Pages with entries","Requests for pronunciation in English entries","Requests for review of Northern Mansi translations","Terms with Arabic translations","Terms with Armenian translations","Terms with Belarusian translations","Terms with Bulgarian translations","Terms with Catalan translations","Terms with Czech translations","Terms with Danish translations","Terms with Dutch tra